The changelog-audit playbook calibrates against the released sections'
detail level. This applies a sharper version of that rule: a word target
derived from commit volume, so the section is sized to the work it
documents rather than to how much was written about it.

  7.0.0    4,992 words / 461 main-only commits = 10.83 words per commit
  6.1.0    2,052 words /  93 main-only commits = 22.06 words per commit

Unreleased covers 290 main-only commits (428 raw, less tracker-package
and .ops-only commits). At the last release's rate that is a target of
**3,140 words**. It stood at 15,750 -- 5.0x its share, and larger than
the last three released sections combined.

Main changelog: 15,750 -> 3,413 words, 129 -> 44 bullets, 122 -> 77 words
per bullet against 7.0.0's 73. Median bullet is 66 words.

- Documentation 41 bullets -> 6. It was a blow-by-blow log of five
  spec/ADR audits and six docstring-staleness passes, narrating each
  pass's internal process; it alone ran 5,874 words, more than all of
  7.0.0 and 18.6x that release's own Documentation section.
- Coverage-directed test selection had one bullet per rollout phase
  (shadow, Phase 2, Phase 3). One feature, one bullet.
- Cache lifecycle 5 -> 2, Security 5 -> 3, verify-claims 5 -> 3, and the
  nine Fixed subheads collapse to eight by folding Release gate together
  with CI and developer workflow.
- "### Fixed -- developer experience" is dissolved. Keep a Changelog has
  fixed categories and the section already had a "### Fixed"; its bullets
  are distributed into the subsections that already owned their topics.
  The section now carries only Summary / Added / Changed / Removed /
  Fixed / Documentation.

Tracker changelog: at 11 commits against 0.7.0's 718 words / 10 commits,
its target is 790 and it now sits at 558 -- already under, so it takes
only the textconv-grouping and concision pass, not a cut.

Twenty-four WI-/INV- tracker identifiers are stripped, per the playbook's
rule that item ids are inside-baseball while ADR references, code
identifiers and version transitions stay.

Verified rather than asserted, against the pre-audit text: all 28 ADR
references, 4 SCHEMA_VERSION constants, 4 PYSEC ids and 5 HYPERGUMBO_*
env vars survive; markup is balanced; both files' released sections are
byte-identical. An intermediate pass dropped 17 ADR references and they
were reinstated in the bullets that inherited their subject matter.

Honest residual: the result is 3,413 against the 3,140 target, 8.7% over.
The overshoot is concentrated in the six Documentation bullets, which
carry all 28 ADR references between them and have a floor the playbook's
preserve-every-reference rule sets. The estimator is also softer than one
figure suggests -- 6.1.0's rate would put the target at 6,399, so the two
reference releases disagree by 2x on words per commit.

